{"type": "message", "message": "**Minimalist means less visual clutter—not less comfort or an empty flat.** It’s a complete, useful home with a restrained mix of furniture, colours and materials.\n\nFor a small flat, that usually means:\n- **Purposeful furniture:** pieces sized for everyday needs, rather than lots of small extras.\n- **Clear routes:** arrange furniture so moving between rooms feels straightforward.\n- **Practical storage:** closed cupboards for everyday clutter, with a few favourite objects on display.\n- **A consistent palette:** a few related colours and finishes; it doesn’t have to be all white.\n- **Warmth through texture:** wood, woven fabrics and a rug can keep simple rooms welcoming.\n\nFor your combined living-and-dining room, the principle would be two clear, coordinated zones—not stripping out the table, lighting or comfortable seating just to leave more floor empty.\n\nI can discuss your flat, but cannot check or change its layout until its geometry is supported.", "conversationId": "db3d3eafd93844b4abf04c878a94f924"}
